> and it's not that sort of book anyway, but what do other people=20
> think?=20
>

If clothbound isn't prohibtively expensive (and it wouldn't make the book=
=20
so), it would also be my preference, without a dust jacket. I would opt for=
=20
the grey cloth, with the idea of reserving blue for the forthcoming=20
major-numbered version.

Aren't there like a *million* more requirements, though, like font=20
> embedding and so on?=20
>

Fonts are embedded as subsets.

The images, as gleki noted in the Art thread=20
<https://groups.google.com/d/msg/lojban/0uyrERTkXsg/rdwNXT6A9eUJ>, are low=
=20
resolution web images: 72 dpi, where 600 dpi is recommended for black and=
=20
white images. When re-sourcing these images, it might be best to select an=
=20
image format that supports black and white: As GIFs, these are RGB images,=
=20
which the guide says will be converted to grayscale, but why not head off=
=20
any potential problem with that?

Regarding the glyph warnings:
=20
=EC=9E=90=EB=AA=A8 turns up in Example 4.51: The literal characters are inc=
luded in the XML.
=EC=9E=90 (U+C790 <http://codepoints.net/U+C790>)
=EB=AA=A8 (U+BAA8 <http://codepoints.net/U+BAA8>)
Font support for JA=20
<http://www.fileformat.info/info/unicode/char/c790/fontsupport.htm> and MO=
=20
<http://www.fileformat.info/info/unicode/char/baa8/fontsupport.htm> is=20
limited. Maybe better to embed them as an image? Or rewrite the example to=
=20
use better supported characters?

=E2=84=B5 is in Example 18.17, as an HTML entity: &#x2135;
=E2=84=B5 (U+2135 <http://codepoints.net/U+2135>)
It seems to be supported in DejaVu Sans=20
<http://www.fileformat.info/info/unicode/char/2135/fontsupport.htm>,=20
although not in DejaVu Serif. Perhaps the entity could be wrapped in a span=
=20
that would style it as sans?

mi'e la mukti mu'o

